[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-122170"] :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-122311/selection/f128d4badfb481f7dcb9847f64d62a512c4343262c483fcea475c36687dbcc38" :answer/id #uuid "d6b1c28a-9dcd-5b52-b85c-14bdbe649dd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122311/selection/1e5b290ccb018f50ad30497e19c1c1e231eb59391f21747f934d507cb96b7eed" :answer/id #uuid "2e836e6e-a238-5f98-b2a7-a4de66e59e13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={2}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122311/selection/1f6e86f0b4e0c812834906a1ac62f1c3310a1ad77df610318d3ceb046437c951" :answer/id #uuid "d2a867e5-d7eb-50c7-a459-f1c9bb83f628" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{2}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122311/selection/584bd20d318cef3f488dcd6fa8ec8a2bccc2715bce1980711c43995f62570fd6" :answer/id #uuid "b11b3c1c-c08c-5c67-94bb-b3871b48b293" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122311/selection/adcda2c7d220d3bf0d35c4915651715417564c26cc2e373737156ca5a0276083" :answer/id #uuid "f1674e45-9cf6-5718-ab2d-c81944cdf046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122311/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b056aa5-8dce-5758-b798-cce33cdc94b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122311/selection/f128d4badfb481f7dcb9847f64d62a512c4343262c483fcea475c36687dbcc38" :answer-field/choices ["q-122311/selection/f128d4badfb481f7dcb9847f64d62a512c4343262c483fcea475c36687dbcc38" "q-122311/selection/1e5b290ccb018f50ad30497e19c1c1e231eb59391f21747f934d507cb96b7eed" "q-122311/selection/1f6e86f0b4e0c812834906a1ac62f1c3310a1ad77df610318d3ceb046437c951" "q-122311/selection/584bd20d318cef3f488dcd6fa8ec8a2bccc2715bce1980711c43995f62570fd6" "q-122311/selection/adcda2c7d220d3bf0d35c4915651715417564c26cc2e373737156ca5a0276083"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122311" :question/id #uuid "61b238ae-9305-5fd3-a3b9-59d2cf1670ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-122311" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${2}^{x}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/b69783c5b926cb22a6e4321b449d006071917618da601d0ba2bfa109ffde6a0f.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${2}^{x}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{2}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122311/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122311"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122341/selection/4fb16683c68c8996bd3f68402bd035631b3a0a6c89eb54ee42f0f4cf8f6fde34" :answer/id #uuid "93ea0098-91e3-5531-951b-dd4214a92746" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{2}^{3-x}+5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122341/selection/69714b7738353736b7d74a62fcca7e8bcc4f456cd126e0fc83ef2e603042b8b7" :answer/id #uuid "943157c1-7ad2-57e2-b1de-cf19aecec9ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v(x)={2}^{1-x}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122341/selection/954efc3a549355a8d8cbcfe6bd1860980db18ecdab515ecf60bf50ba7f303497" :answer/id #uuid "2150f8e0-e628-5e90-9f6f-342069ff1e29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v(x)={2}^{3-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122341/selection/ae29b3e3f114a233f13d48027d785849432d251b58909abe66b6a6404b9613d8" :answer/id #uuid "4118df9f-1946-565b-8bfe-c8a8ef7e1930" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122341/selection/336a666c251fdf4fef8e14108b90ff90a969699b482e31ec3f03676a823e4ae8" :answer/id #uuid "746159af-834d-5499-a659-8fdbd26fe75d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122341/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c6021a-dd65-597b-8adf-5b6819472e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122341/selection/336a666c251fdf4fef8e14108b90ff90a969699b482e31ec3f03676a823e4ae8" :answer-field/choices ["q-122341/selection/4fb16683c68c8996bd3f68402bd035631b3a0a6c89eb54ee42f0f4cf8f6fde34" "q-122341/selection/69714b7738353736b7d74a62fcca7e8bcc4f456cd126e0fc83ef2e603042b8b7" "q-122341/selection/954efc3a549355a8d8cbcfe6bd1860980db18ecdab515ecf60bf50ba7f303497" "q-122341/selection/ae29b3e3f114a233f13d48027d785849432d251b58909abe66b6a6404b9613d8" "q-122341/selection/336a666c251fdf4fef8e14108b90ff90a969699b482e31ec3f03676a823e4ae8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122341" :question/id #uuid "a6d10a0c-9eaf-5b8d-b65b-c7a48f4d43e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122341" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/6ddbfef68a0b41a6ee3f11c46ff7821f7ff0dc23a16b4244b5e7a1f6579d7dd2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={2}^{2-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2-1}+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={2}^{3-x}+12 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({2}^{1-x}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-122341/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122341"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-122343"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-122345"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/634a0680d0f159006c524b8d0eb466b2c99b0f9b5a46cf38b7cd689c9d8d6fb2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=12\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=7\\,\\text{m}$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(12)^{2} & =\\frac{9(7+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ 72 & =72+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =9(x+1)^{4/3} \\\\ v & =\\pm 3(x+1)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3(x+1)^{2/3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=7$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x+1}=t+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+2 \\\\ x+1 & =(t+2)^{3} \\\\ x & =(t+2)^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
